# L §4.4 Each operation gives the interval of its values (tally 2.1.7 R3, issue #142; F4): add_business and
# is_business aren't monotone in their day, so with a window argument they are evaluated for each day it can take:
# the interval of the results (window(2026-06-02, 2026-06-08)), or one value where they agree (true on the weekdays
# w can be, false on u's weekend); dcf is monotone in each day, so its ends give it, and dcf(…) > 0 is decided.
# 2.1.6's refcore made each a hole.
=== m.tally
model w
  tally 2
  on 2026-10-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

index qs = [q1, q2, q3]

input w: date

input wd: date

input u: date

input cal: calendar

input d[k in qs]: date

input rank[k in qs]: int

def same: date = w

def paid: date = w + 1 day

ask a4: add_business(w, 1, cal), is_business(w, cal), is_business(u, cal), dcf(w, 2026-12-31, act365) > 0

version v
  cite card
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  w = window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-05) "c"
  wd = window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-01) "c"
  u = window(2026-06-06, 2026-06-07) "c"
  cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) "c"
  d[q1] = 2026-03-01 "c"
  d[q2] = 2026-03-01 "c"
  d[q3] = window(2026-04-01, 2026-04-30) "c"
  rank[q1] = 2 "c"
  rank[q2] = 1 "c"
  rank[q3] = 0 "c"
=== expect a4
a4: (window(2026-06-02, 2026-06-08), true, false, true)
...
